NNOOTTEESS
       For  a  discussion  on the effects of an exit, the transmission of exit
       status, zombie processes, signals sent, and so on, see eexxiitt(3).

       The function __eexxiitt() is like eexxiitt(3), but does not call  any  functions
       registered with aatteexxiitt(3) or oonn__eexxiitt(3).  Open ssttddiioo(3) streams are not
       flushed.   On the other hand, __eexxiitt() does close open file descriptors,
       and this may cause an unknown delay, waiting for pending output to fin‐
       ish.  If the delay is undesired, it may be  useful  to  call  functions
       like  ttccfflluusshh(3)  before  calling  __eexxiitt().  Whether any pending I/O is
       canceled, and which pending I/O may be canceled upon __eexxiitt(), is imple‐
       mentation‐dependent.

   CC lliibbrraarryy//kkeerrnneell ddiiffffeerreenncceess
       The text above in  DESCRIPTION  describes  the  traditional  effect  of
       __eexxiitt(),  which  is to terminate a process, and these are the semantics
       specified by POSIX.1 and implemented by the C library wrapper function.
       On modern systems,  this  means  termination  of  all  threads  in  the
       process.

       By  contrast with the C library wrapper function, the raw Linux __eexxiitt()
       system call terminates only the calling thread, and actions such as re‐
       parenting child processes or sending SSIIGGCCHHLLDD to the parent process  are
       performed only if this is the last thread in the thread group.

       Up to glibc 2.3, the __eexxiitt() wrapper function invoked the kernel system
       call  of  the same name.  Since glibc 2.3, the wrapper function invokes
       eexxiitt__ggrroouupp(2), in order to terminate all of the threads in a process.

